# UpAgent Recruiter launch templates — PUBLIC TEMPLATE.
#
# Copy to `.shared-llm/this_repo/extensions/common/upagent/upagent.yaml` only when a destination
# still has legacy route/controller launch profiles, then adapt those explicitly non-public
# commands. The filled roster is repo-owned and never overwritten by `just update`; this example
# ships from the kit only as its starting point.
#
# The roster is keyed by HARNESS, not by worker type or persona. route.yaml is authoritative
# for which harness/model/agent runs each stage; the Recruiter only knows HOW to launch each
# harness as a Herdr pane. It never decides the agent.
#
# WHO can be asked — the specialist roster a consult routes by — is a different question with a
# different lifecycle. Its kit base is
# `.shared-llm/public/extensions/common/upagent/specialists.yaml`; a destination overlay lives at
# `.shared-llm/this_repo/extensions/common/upagent/specialists.yaml`. Launch templates REPLACE
# across base/legacy configuration; specialists MERGE. Do not put a `specialists:` block here.
#
# Each template is a Python str.format() string. Available placeholders:
#   {model}             resolved model id from the route llm_profile (the route schema requires
#                       a model per profile, so this is never empty on a route-driven order —
#                       which matters, because a flag like `--model {model}` handed an empty
#                       value would make the CLI eat the next token as the model)
#   {agent}             the persona/agent name from the route stage entry
#   {effort}            reasoning effort from the route llm_profile — the leader resolves it
#                       to `medium` when the profile omits it, so it is never empty
#   {cwd}               the phase worktree (also set as the pane's --cwd)
#   {instructions_path} absolute path to the stage brief the worker must read
#   {result_path}       absolute path the worker MUST write result.json to
#   {order_id}          exact order identity the worker MUST echo in result.json
#
# Model-id shapes differ per harness: claude takes an alias or full name (`opus`,
# `claude-opus-4-8`) plus `--effort low|medium|high|xhigh|max`; Codex takes a bare model id
# (`gpt-5.6-sol`) plus `-c model_reasoning_effort={effort}`; pi takes a provider-qualified
# `provider/id` plus explicit `--thinking {effort}`.
#
# IMPORTANT: because templates are str.format() strings, any LITERAL brace must be DOUBLED —
# write `${{VAR}}` for a shell variable and `{{a,b}}` for a literal brace group. A single
# unescaped `{...}` that is not one of the placeholders above is a template authoring error.
#
# Every worker's job is the same shape: read the instructions, do the one stage, write a
# valid result.json — verdict exactly one of passed|failed|blocked; revisit ONLY on a failed
# verdict and ALWAYS a JSON list of recognized stage ids (never a bare string, never prose);
# full_log a pointer to the session transcript — and then GO IDLE. A worker never closes its
# own pane: the Recruiter validates the staged files while the worker is still addressable,
# may send one COMPLETION_REPAIR turn to fix them, and closes the pane itself afterward.
# Codex exec exits by construction and uses reader-side recovery instead of a live repair.
# Claude, Pi, and Cursor are interactive: Herdr `done` means only one turn ended, so the
# Recruiter ignores it for terminality and waits for a valid typed bundle, proven pane/process
# exit, or the deadline. Missing or malformed bundles get one same-address repair only after
# that legitimate boundary.
#
# UNATTENDED STARTUP IS MANDATORY. A worker runs AFK in a pane, so every launch template MUST
# bypass all trust/permission prompts or the worker hangs forever at a dialog (and the Recruiter's
# wait times out). Interactive TUIs stay visible; unattended does not mean headless. The bypass:
#   claude → --dangerously-skip-permissions             (trusts cwd, incl. a fresh worktree)
#   codex  → --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox (with --skip-git-repo-check)
#   pi     → --approve                                  (trusts project-local files for the run)
#   cursor → --force --trust                            (approve commands + trust workspace)
#
# PI WORKERS RUN INSULATED. `--no-extensions` turns off extension discovery so a broken
# globally-installed extension can never brick orchestrated workers (a half-installed
# extension once rerouted every bash/read call through a missing binary — every worker
# died on its first tool call). Explicit `-e` paths still load, and one is REQUIRED:
# Herdr's pi integration (`herdr integration install pi` puts it at
# $HOME/.pi/agent/extensions/herdr-agent-state.ts) reports live working/idle/blocked
# supervision state. Turn-level `done` is UI telemetry, not completion authority; typed
# artifacts are authoritative. Interactive pi sessions keep the full extension set.
# Note pi has NO --agent flag: the
# persona comes to the worker through the stage brief, not the CLI.

# LEGACY/NON-PUBLIC LLM lifecycle roles are separate from work routes. They interpret
# configuration and bounded health evidence; Python remains the only pane/process executor. Role
# commands may use {brief_path}, {cwd}, and {output_path}. Public façade requests NEVER use these
# raw commands: they always load `offerings.yaml`, whose code-owned renderer applies the ordered
# Account Manager, Checker, and Sentinel candidates declared there, even when this file exists.
#
# mode: direct (legacy route default) — route/controller orders have no standing manager unless
# their order opts in. Python still owns lease, validation, publication, and terminality; any
# manager is advisory and a manager crash degrades supervision rather than blocking a mechanically
# valid worker. An explicitly legacy raw order may opt in with
# `"management": {"mode": "dedicated"}`. Legacy consult-door orders follow this roster default.
#
# rescue_on_startup_failure (default true) — when a worker launch fails or stalls before
# health verification, hire one bounded broker (the checker role) to read the failure and
# advise retry vs stop, then relaunch at most once. If the broker itself cannot run, the
# Recruiter retries once anyway. Applies in both modes.
management:
  mode: direct
  rescue_on_startup_failure: true
  startup_timeout_ms: 45000
  inactivity_check_ms: 900000
  requester_grace_ms: 300000
  account_manager:
    command: >-
      claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --agent upagent-account-manager
      --model claude-sonnet-5 --effort low
      "Read {brief_path}, perform that one lifecycle review, write {output_path},
      then remain available."
    expected_agent: claude
    expected_process: claude
    timeout_ms: 120000
  checker:
    command: >-
      claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --agent upagent-checker
      --model haiku --effort low
      "Read {brief_path}, perform that one bounded assessment, write {output_path}, then exit."
    expected_agent: claude
    expected_process: claude
    timeout_ms: 120000
  # Hired ONLY when a vanished worker leaves CONTRADICTORY evidence — a staged result.json
  # that will not validate, or a ledger recording artifacts while staging is empty. Python
  # decides clean hits (a commit landed after the order started) and clean misses (nothing on
  # disk) by itself and never spends a model on them.
  #
  # The Rescuer is advisory: it reads a bounded evidence bundle (ledger tail, staging listing,
  # git log of the order worktree) and returns one typed JSON verdict —
  # salvageable-done | truly-blocked | rerun — plus cited commits and files. The runner accepts
  # `salvageable-done` only after mechanically re-verifying EVERY citation (the SHA must resolve
  # in that worktree, the file must exist and parse). An uncorroborated citation is discarded,
  # the order stays blocked, and the receipt records `rescuer_uncorroborated`. An LLM claim can
  # never mark work done here.
  #
  # A salvaged terminal publishes verdict `salvaged-done`, never `passed`, with
  # `confirmation: unconfirmed`; the receipt's `synthesis_path` says which route produced it
  # (clean | salvaged-mechanical | salvaged-rescuer).
  rescuer:
    command: >-
      claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --agent upagent-rescuer
      --model claude-sonnet-5 --effort low
      "Read {brief_path}, perform that one bounded salvage assessment, write {output_path},
      then exit."
    expected_agent: claude
    expected_process: claude
    timeout_ms: 120000
  # One per sentinel-supervised request (default-on; `--no-sentinel` / order `sentinel: false`
  # opts out): a cheap pane duty-bound to that request's worker from liftoff to closeout.
  # Eyes and interpretation only — it holds no kill switch, its closeout citations are
  # re-verified in Python before they count, and a claimed COMPLETE still passes the ordinary
  # bundle validation before anything publishes.
  sentinel:
    command: >-
      claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --agent upagent-sentinel
      --model haiku --effort low
      "Read {brief_path}, perform that one sentinel duty cycle for its worker, write
      {output_path} when the worker lifecycle ends, then exit."
    expected_agent: claude
    expected_process: claude
    timeout_ms: 120000
# Request intake (no configuration): every NEW REQUEST carrying a `cockpit_pane` has that pane
# verified with herdr before ledger creation or launch. A missing pane is refused immediately.
# An identical ad-hoc/public retry may refresh only this routing field before Recruiter acceptance
# (`just upagent up`, then retry the same id; optionally pass the current `--cockpit-pane`). Once
# accepted, the exact pane is frozen and attachment needs no live caller pane. Relaunching a phase
# leader changes its pane id — re-point the run's phase-start.json with
# `just leader-restamp <phase-start.json> <new-pane>`. The restamp only
# verifies the given pane is LIVE with herdr and stamps herdr's own canonical id for it; herdr
# exposes no leader-ownership field on a pane record, so it cannot also prove the pane belongs
# to THIS leader — the command trusts the operator's pane id once liveness is confirmed.
#
# Optional process-detector overrides. Use these when a harness wrapper changes what Herdr reports.
# A mismatch fails startup; the Hub never guesses that some unrelated foreground process is healthy.
health:
  claude:
    expected_agent: claude
    expected_process: claude
  codex:
    expected_agent: codex
    expected_process: codex
  pi:
    expected_agent: pi
    expected_process: pi
  cursor:
    expected_agent: cursor
    expected_process: cursor-agent

# Phase leaders are controllers, not stage workers. The deterministic phase-start transaction
# holds this command behind a filesystem gate, writes the durable leader receipt, verifies leader
# health, then releases the leader. Coordination v2 has no standing phase watchdog. Keeping
# separate templates prevents the controller from trying to trim a worker prompt or guess each
# harness's controller mode.
# The claude leader deliberately does NOT pass `--agent {agent}`: no phase-leader agent
# definition ships anywhere, so the flag pointed claude at a nonexistent file and it exited
# on the spot (surfacing as a confusing pane_not_found at phase start). The persona name
# still travels in the prompt; --model and --effort stay on the command line, always.
phase_leaders:
  claude: >-
    claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --model {model} --effort {effort}
    "Read {instructions_path} and follow that phase-leader assignment as the {agent} persona."

  codex: >-
    codex exec --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox --skip-git-repo-check
    --model {model} -c model_reasoning_effort={effort}
    "Read {instructions_path} and follow that phase-leader assignment."

  pi: >-
    pi --approve --no-extensions -e "$HOME/.pi/agent/extensions/herdr-agent-state.ts"
    --model {model} --thinking {effort}
    "Read {instructions_path} and follow that phase-leader assignment."

  cursor: >-
    cursor-agent --force --trust --model {model}
    "Read {instructions_path} and follow that phase-leader assignment."

harnesses:
  claude: >-
    claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --agent {agent} --model {model} --effort {effort}
    "Read {instructions_path} and do exactly that one stage in {cwd}.
    When finished, write exactly one result.json to {result_path} with order_id exactly {order_id}.
    verdict: exactly one of passed|failed|blocked. revisit: only on a failed verdict, and always
    a JSON list of recognized stage ids from the brief — never a bare string, never prose.
    full_log: a pointer to your session transcript. Then remain in your session and go idle;
    do NOT exit or close your pane — the Recruiter validates your files and closes the pane."

  # Codex has no --agent flag. The persona comes from instructions_path. Its exec process and
  # token-scoped completion monitor, rather than agent-status, delimit the run and finalize its
  # private staged result without any Codex-specific public-result polling.
  # codex exec exits when the run ends (it cannot stay idle), so its recovery path is Python's
  # reader-side repair plus the completion reactor, never a live COMPLETION_REPAIR turn.
  codex: >-
    codex exec --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox --skip-git-repo-check
    --model {model} -c model_reasoning_effort={effort}
    "Read {instructions_path} and do exactly that one stage in {cwd}.
    Write exactly one result.json to {result_path} with order_id exactly {order_id}.
    verdict: exactly one of passed|failed|blocked. revisit: only on a failed verdict, and always
    a JSON list of recognized stage ids from the brief — never a bare string, never prose.
    full_log: a pointer to your session transcript."

  pi: >-
    pi --approve --no-extensions -e "$HOME/.pi/agent/extensions/herdr-agent-state.ts"
    --model {model} --thinking {effort}
    "Read {instructions_path}; do the stage in {cwd}; write exactly one result.json to
    {result_path} with order_id exactly {order_id}.
    verdict: exactly one of passed|failed|blocked. revisit: only on a failed verdict, and always
    a JSON list of recognized stage ids from the brief — never a bare string, never prose.
    full_log: a pointer to your session transcript. Then remain in your session and go idle;
    do NOT exit or close your pane — the Recruiter validates your files and closes the pane."

  # Cursor stays interactive so Herdr shows live progress. Turn-level done is not terminal; an
  # absent bundle remains supervised until artifacts, positively confirmed process/pane exit, or
  # the deadline, after which missing or malformed required artifacts can receive one repair.
  cursor: >-
    cursor-agent --force --trust --model {model}
    "Read {instructions_path}; do the stage in {cwd}; write exactly one result.json to
    {result_path} with order_id exactly {order_id}.
    verdict: exactly one of passed|failed|blocked. revisit: only on a failed verdict, and always
    a JSON list of recognized stage ids from the brief — never a bare string, never prose.
    full_log: a pointer to your session transcript. Then remain in your session and go idle;
    do NOT exit or close your pane — the Recruiter validates your files and closes the pane."
